  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nThe busy weather trend continues, and focus efforts has \nprimarily been with the first 24-36 hours of the forecast. \n\n\nFlooding Concerns:\n\nThe earlier rainfall has finally moved out, and the Flood Watch\nfor east central Illinois was cancelled. \n\nRainfall amounts of 4 to 6 inches were fairly widespread from \nthe I-74 corridor and then southward in east central Illinois to\nI-70. While the earlier watch from Havana-Bloomington northward\nwas allowed to expire at 10 am, a new watch has been issued for\nthis area beginning at 10 pm tonight. The next MCS is expected \nto develop across Iowa this evening, as an upper wave moves \nacross that area, and then move into Illinois shortly after \nmidnight. HREF precipitable water values over 2.2 inches remain \nin place overnight across the northern third of Illinois, and \nprobabilities of over 2 inches of rain tonight are highest from \nGalesburg to Lacon (40-60%). However, its LPMM guidance suggests\n3-5 inches are a valid concern, depending on where exactly the \nMCS tracks. While the flooding is the main focus with this MCS, \nthere remains a level 2 severe weather threat with it, primarily\nfor scattered damaging winds.\n\n\nHeat Concerns:\n\nExtensive rain today has significantly kept temperatures lower\nthan earlier projections. While skies are now becoming partly\nsunny south of I-72, it is unclear whether enough sunshine will\nget heat index readings up to 100 degrees in any of the advisory\narea today. The current advisory area is expected to have less\nimpact from the overnight MCS, and guidance continues to point\ntoward heat index values 105-110 degrees on Saturday and Sunday.\nNo changes in the Heat Advisory were made in that area for this\nreason. Further north, NBM guidance is also showing similar \nheat index for Saturday for all of central Illinois. However, \nthe lingering effects of the next MCS suggest the guidance is \noverdoing projections. HREF probabilities of greater than 105 \ndegree heat index on Saturday are highest from Beardstown \nsoutheast through Taylorville to Marshall (40-60%), and tail off\npretty quickly north of there. Will leave any headline changes \nfor the overnight forecast cycle, once the MCS shows its hand.",
